      {
        "narration": "Dev is baking muffins. The recipe needs 2/3 cup of milk. But his measuring cup only has marks for sixths. Can he still measure the right amount?",
        "text": "2/3 cup",
        "visual": { "kind": "emoji", "emoji": "🥛", "count": null, "filled": null }
      },
      {
        "narration": "Dev splits each third into 2 equal parts. The cup now has 6 parts, so 2 thirds becomes 4 sixths. The amount of milk is the same. Only the number of parts changed.",
        "text": "2/3 = 4/6",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "narration": "Here is the rule. Multiply the numerator and the denominator by the same number, and you get an equivalent fraction. Divide both by the same number, and you get one too.",
        "text": "2 × 2 = 4 and 3 × 2 = 6",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "narration": "Next, Dev needs 7 scoops of flour. Each scoop is 1/4 cup. That is 7/4 cups. Seven fourths is an improper fraction, because the numerator is bigger than the denominator.",
        "text": "7/4",
        "visual": { "kind": "objects", "emoji": "🥄", "count": 7, "filled": null }
      },
      {
        "narration": "Every 4 fourths make 1 whole cup. Divide 7 by 4. You get 1 whole, with 3 fourths left over. So 7/4 is equal to the mixed number 1 3/4.",
        "text": "7 ÷ 4 = 1 remainder 3, so 7/4 = 1 3/4",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "narration": "To go back, multiply the whole number by the denominator, then add the numerator. For 1 3/4, that is 1 × 4 + 3 = 7. So the improper fraction is 7/4.",
        "text": "1 × 4 + 3 = 7",
        "visual": null
      },
      {
        "narration": "Now you can switch between equivalent fractions, improper fractions, and mixed numbers. Let's practise with Dev's kitchen.",
        "text": null,
        "visual": null
      }
    ],
